// MTEngineSDL -- TRACKED engine build settings.
//
// This is NOT the generated MTEngineCaps.xcconfig. Two files, one job each, and
// three earlier drafts of the plan used one name for both:
//
//   * THIS file is tracked, hand-written, and named by the engine target's
//     baseConfigurationReference. It holds the engine's OWN defaults -- the ones
//     a developer gets when they clone the engine and open it in Xcode with no
//     app in sight -- plus one line that consumes the wrapper's settings.
//
//   * $MT_OUT/MTEngineCaps.xcconfig is GENERATED and is imported by nothing. Its
//     path ends in a capability hash, so no tracked baseConfigurationReference
//     could ever name it. build-macos.sh READS it and turns it into xcodebuild
//     settings.
//
// MEASURED (spike G, 2026-08-23):
//
//   * $(MT_CAPS_DEFINES) DOES expand here when the value is supplied on the
//     xcodebuild command line -- question 9, and the whole mechanism rests on it;
//   * a target's own buildSettings OUTRANK its own xcconfig, so the define list
//     had to MOVE here rather than be added alongside;
//   * a command-line setting cannot be scoped per-target and does not need to be:
//     it reaches every target in the invocation, and the split is made HERE, by
//     which settings this file chooses to reference. MT_CAPS_DEFINES_ENGINE is
//     referenced by this file and by no app's, so a flag no app guards on stays
//     out of app translation units.

// Platform defines. NOT capabilities and never emitted by mtcaps: MACOS has 74
// sites in the engine, many with no __APPLE__ fallback, and the generator has no
// source for it.
// Basis Universal transcoder configuration. NOT capabilities either: these say
// what the VENDORED transcoder is compiled to support, and MT_ENABLE_KTX2 is the
// separate question of whether the engine uses it. Stated rather than inherited
// because basisu_transcoder.h defaults them to 1 in its own #ifndef -- so KTX2
// worked here by a vendored library's default, and the next resync of that
// library could flip it with nothing in this repository to object. See the
// matching static_asserts in CImageData.cpp.
MT_BASIS_DEFINES = BASISD_SUPPORT_KTX2=1 BASISD_SUPPORT_KTX2_ZSTD=1

GCC_PREPROCESSOR_DEFINITIONS = $(inherited) MACOS $(MT_BASIS_DEFINES) $(MT_CAPS_DEFINES) $(MT_CAPS_DEFINES_ENGINE) $(MT_ENGINE_DEFAULT_CAPS)

// The engine's own capability defaults, for STANDALONE use only.
//
// "Clone the engine, open it, build it" is the first thing an external
// contributor does, and MTEngineSDL ships on public GitHub -- a design where that
// fails with "run some app's wrapper" is not shippable. So when no wrapper has
// supplied MT_CAPS_DEFINES, these are what the engine compiles with.
//
// The wrapper leaves MT_ENGINE_DEFAULT_CAPS empty, so an app build gets its own
// manifest's set and nothing else. Standalone leaves MT_CAPS_DEFINES empty and
// gets this list.
MT_ENGINE_DEFAULT_CAPS = $(MT_ENGINE_DEFAULT_CAPS_$(MT_CAPS_SUPPLIED))
MT_CAPS_SUPPLIED = NO
MT_ENGINE_DEFAULT_CAPS_NO = MT_ENABLE_IMGUI_TEST_ENGINE=1 MT_ENABLE_MBEDTLS=1 MT_ENABLE_FTXUI=1 MT_CAMERA_CAPTURE_ENABLED=1 MT_ENABLE_LIBTIFF=1 MT_ENABLE_LIBWEBP=1 MT_ENABLE_LIBAVIF=1 MT_ENABLE_LIBRAW=1 MT_ENABLE_FFMPEG=1 MT_ENABLE_WEBM_VPX=1 MT_ENABLE_LLAMA_CPP=1 MT_ENABLE_LCMS2=1 MT_ENABLE_HTTPLIB=1 MT_ENABLE_ENET=1 MT_ENABLE_RTMIDI=1 MT_ENABLE_GAMEPADS=1 MT_ENABLE_UNDO=1 MT_ENABLE_CRASH_REPORTER=1 MT_ENABLE_LIBTMT=1 MT_ENABLE_LIBHEIF=1 MT_ENABLE_KTX2=1 MT_ENABLE_UWEBSOCKETS=1
MT_ENGINE_DEFAULT_CAPS_YES =

// $MT_OUT/include, where MT_Capabilities.h and llama_cpp_version.h land. On a
// WRAPPER build the wrapper passes it as HEADER_SEARCH_PATHS with $(inherited) --
// measured: a BARE assignment replaces the engine's own list wholesale, which is
// substantial, so it would break every include and not merely the capability
// header.
//
// This line is for the STANDALONE build, which has no wrapper to pass anything.
// llama_cpp_version.h used to be a TRACKED file in src/Engine/Sci/Llama/ that
// build-llama_cpp.sh rewrote on every build; it now lands under the generated
// include dir, and CGuiViewLlamaModelLoader.cpp includes it unconditionally, so
// a bare `xcodebuild -scheme MTEngineSDL` needs that directory on the path.
//
// It is set on the TARGET, not here, and that is this project's own spike result
// applied to itself: a target's own buildSettings OUTRANK its own xcconfig, so a
// HEADER_SEARCH_PATHS line in this file would be silently inert -- measured, it
// was. The defines could move here because nothing else set them; the search
// paths could not, because the target's list is substantial and stays.
//
// The default root only. A standalone build with MTENGINE_BUILD_ROOT or
// XDG_CACHE_HOME set must pass HEADER_SEARCH_PATHS itself: an xcconfig cannot
// evaluate a shell fallback chain, and inventing a second expression of the same
// rule is how two copies drift.

// WHERE DEPENDENCY ARCHIVES LIVE, and it is not in this checkout.
//
// The six acquisition phases used to stage libSDL3.a, libllama_cpp.a and the
// rest into platform/MacOS/libs/ -- inside the repository, shared by four apps,
// and DIFFERENT PER CAPABILITY SET, so two apps with different manifests fought
// over one archive. That fight is what the build lock was serializing.
//
// $(MT_CAPS_OUT) is supplied by both channels (the wrapper on the command line,
// the pre-action through the include below) and is already keyed by app, engine
// revision, platform, arch, config, licence mode, backend and capability hash.
// The fallback is the standalone root the acquisition scripts use when no
// manifest exists -- see mt_caps_lib_dir in platform/caps-lib.sh, which is the
// single definition this line mirrors.
// The STANDALONE FALLBACK only. Both real channels override it: the CLI wrapper
// passes MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR on the xcodebuild command line (which outranks every
// xcconfig), and the IDE pre-action writes it into the generated xcconfig this
// file #include?s below (which is read after this assignment, so it wins).
//
// This value therefore applies to exactly one case -- opening the engine on its
// own with no manifest anywhere -- and it must agree with mt_caps_lib_dir's own
// fallback in platform/caps-lib.sh, since that is what the acquisition scripts
// use in the same situation. An xcconfig cannot read $MTENGINE_BUILD_ROOT or
// $XDG_CACHE_HOME, so the default location is spelled out literally; overriding
// either variable means using one of the two real channels.
MT_CAPS_LIBS_DIR = $(HOME)/.cache/mtengine/_deps/standalone/libs

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE IDE CHANNEL.
//
// Written by the scheme's build PRE-ACTION (tools/mtcaps/xcode-preaction.sh) at
// the start of every Xcode build, and OUTSIDE every checkout. It is what makes
// an IDE Build or Archive honour mtengine.caps rather than the engine's
// defaults -- without it, Archive could ship libraries the manifest turns off.
//
// `#include?`, not `#include`: a missing file degrades SILENTLY, so a developer
// who has never run the pre-action still gets a working build (falling back to
// the defaults below, with the in-target backstop deciding whether that is
// acceptable for this manifest).
//
// Measured: a pre-action's write takes effect in the SAME build, and an absolute
// #include? resolves.
//
// A command-line setting still beats this, so the script path is never a VICTIM
// of a stale file here: the wrapper's values win and the two agree anyway.
//
// It IS an aggressor, though, and that surprised the design: MEASURED,
// `xcodebuild -scheme` runs the scheme's pre-action too, so a script build
// rewrites this shared file exactly like an IDE build does. Concurrent builds of
// DIFFERENT apps therefore serialize on tools/mtcaps/build-lock.sh.
//
//
// THE PATH IS RELATIVE, and it has to be. Measured: $(HOME) and build settings
// generally do NOT expand inside an #include? path -- only a literal absolute
// path works there, and hardcoding one is both forbidden here and wrong for
// anyone else's machine. A relative path resolves from THIS file's directory,
// which lands on `.mtengine-ide/` beside the five repos: inside none of them,
// and requiring only the sibling layout the whole programme already assumes.
//
// IT MUST COME LAST IN THIS FILE. In an xcconfig a LATER assignment wins, so
// an include placed above the fallback defaults is silently overwritten by
// them -- measured: MT_CAPS_SUPPLIED stayed NO with the include at the top,
// and the IDE build compiled against the defaults while the pre-action had
// written the correct file.
#include? "../../../.mtengine-ide/_current/engine.xcconfig"

